To create a thread, pass a callable object (function, lambda, functor) to the `std::thread` constructor. To wait for its completion, call `join()` on the `std::thread` object.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

void task() {
    std::cout << "Hello from thread!\n";
}

int main() {
    std::thread myThread(task);
    myThread.join(); // Wait for myThread to finish
    std::cout << "Main thread finished.\n";
    return 0;
}
```

By default, arguments passed to a `std::thread`'s function are **copied** into internal storage, even if the function signature takes them by reference. This prevents dangling references if the original argument goes out of scope.

To explicitly pass an argument by reference, use `std::ref()` (from `<functional>`):

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <functional> // For std::ref

void increment(int& x) { x++; }

int main() {
    int val = 0;
    std::thread t(increment, std::ref(val));
    t.join();
    std::cout << "Value after thread: " << val << " (expected 1)\n";
    return 0;
}
```
